"""
pipeline_analyzer_v7.py

Produces a DEEP PER-SCRIPT SUMMARY for each script in a numbered Python pipeline,
in a fixed five-part structure:

    1. Purpose         - one plain-English sentence (LLM-written if Ollama is up)
    2. Inputs          - files the script reads
    3. Outputs         - files the script writes
    4. Calculations    - the actual math: arithmetic on columns, np.where, trig,
                         groupby aggregations, unit conversions, .apply()
    5. Data shaping    - merges, filters, row add/drop, renames, missing-value
                         handling, reshapes (melt/pivot), formatting

Plus an "open items" box pulled from TODO/FIXME/"need to" comments.

DETERMINISTIC CORE + OPTIONAL LOCAL LLM
  The bulk of every summary is extracted deterministically by parsing the code -
  no model needed. If a local Ollama server is running, v7 additionally asks it
  to (a) write the one-sentence Purpose and (b) phrase the calculation and
  shaping findings as readable prose. If Ollama is NOT running, v7 still produces
  the full structured summary - it just skips the prose polish. Nothing ever
  leaves the machine: the only network call is to http://localhost:11434.

USAGE
    # structured summary only, no LLM
    python pipeline_analyzer_v7.py .\\ATHENA_Scripts -o script_summaries.md

    # with local Ollama prose polish (qwen2.5-coder:7b recommended)
    python pipeline_analyzer_v7.py .\\ATHENA_Scripts -o script_summaries.md --llm

    # pick a different local model
    python pipeline_analyzer_v7.py .\\ATHENA_Scripts -o script_summaries.md \\
        --llm --model qwen2.5-coder:3b

SETUP FOR THE --llm OPTION
    1. Install Ollama (https://ollama.com)
    2. ollama pull qwen2.5-coder:7b
    3. Make sure `ollama serve` is running (the desktop app does this for you)
    4. Run v7 with --llm

LLM results are cached in `.summary_cache.json` next to the output, keyed by a
hash of each script's contents, so re-runs only call the model for changed files.
"""

